Z||000005||Seanh||06-11-2001||02:40 PM||||Yes, I was told by a GM dealer that if I purchased a 3.4L Crate engine that drilling the starter holes on the engine would void the warrenty. I guess I can understand why, but I didn't go that route, just bought a Camaro engine instead. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||p4n1c||06-11-2001||02:52 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Dave Gunsul:<BR><B>All multi-point injected 2.8 engines used the 1.72 intake and 1.42 exhaust valves just like the Fiero.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yup, mine's basically the same engine, except with DIS, power steering, more restrictive exhaust and intake.  <P>I'm pretty sure if I overhaul both of those, it'll be the same performance as the Fiero engine, except the car weighs about 400lbs more.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>-=-=-=[ p4n1c ]=-=-=-<BR>1988 Olds Cutlass Supreme International<BR>2.8L MPFI V6/TH440-T4 OD auto<BR>"Ride the geriatric mobile.. err..  rocket"||||reg||1
Z||000007||rockcrawl||06-11-2001||03:57 PM||||I believe Dave is correct on the valve issue, all fuel injected 2.8s have the big valves.<BR> I just sold an '87 GT with an Autozone 2.8 engine in it. It was installed in an Autozone manager's Fiero until he overheated it and destroyed a piston. I yanked it out, repaired it, and put it back in a different Fiero. Maybe they don't warrant them in Fieros because they are more prone to overheating than the 'average' car.||||reg||1
Z||000008||FieroHarry||06-12-2001||01:09 AM||||Nah it just scared them, You know "They made them their fieros funny like".<BR>Damn how many times have I heard that one <P>------------------<BR><B>Harry Waggoner</B><BR>1985 GT 264K+ <I>FOCOSEVA,FOCOA</I><BR><B>"Im not driving to fast, Im flying to low!"</B>||||reg||8
